      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 American Academy of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Mosby, Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Endogenous prostanoids have been suggested to modulate sensitization during experimental allergic asthma, but the specific role of prostaglandin (PG) E\u2082 or of specific E prostanoid (EP) receptors is not known."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Here we tested the role of EP2 signaling in allergic asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Wild-type (WT) and EP2(-/-) mice were subjected to ovalbumin sensitization and acute airway challenge. The PGE2 analog misoprostol was administered during sensitization in both genotypes. In vitro culture of splenocytes and flow-sorted dendritic cells and T cells defined the mechanism by which EP2 exerted its protective effect. Adoptive transfer of WT and EP2(-/-) CD4 T cells was used to validate the importance of EP2 expression on T cells."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Compared with WT mice, EP2(-/-) mice had exaggerated airway inflammation in this model. Splenocytes and lung lymph node cells from sensitized EP2(-/-) mice produced more IL-13 than did WT cells, suggesting increased sensitization. In WT but not EP2(-/-) mice, subcutaneous administration of misoprostol during sensitization inhibited allergic inflammation. PGE\u2082 decreased cytokine production and inhibited signal transducer and activator of transcription 6 phosphorylation by CD3/CD28-stimulated CD4(+) T cells. Coculture of flow cytometry-sorted splenic CD4(+) T cells and CD11c(+) dendritic cells from WT or EP2(-/-) mice suggested that the increased IL-13 production in EP2(-/-) mice was due to the lack of EP2 specifically on T cells. Adoptive transfer of CD4(+) EP2(-/-) T cells caused greater cytokine production in the lungs of WT mice than did transfer of WT CD4(+) T cells."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "We conclude that the PGE2-EP2 axis is an important endogenous brake on allergic airway inflammation and primarily targets T cells and that its agonism represents a potential novel therapeutic approach to asthma."
          }
        ]
      },
